📌 What is the Push Event?
The push event is a GitHub Actions trigger that automatically starts a workflow whenever changes are pushed to a GitHub repository.
We can also configure it to run only when changes are pushed to specific branches, such as main or develop.
It is commonly used to automate build processes, run tests, and validate code changes.

🛠️ Practical Example:
name: Push Event Demo
  on:
   push:
     branches:
       - main

  jobs:
   demo:
     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
     steps:
       - name: Checkout Repository
         uses: actions/checkout@v4

        - name: Display Message
         run: echo "Code pushed to main branch!"

        - name: Check Repository Files
         run: ls -la

🔍 How it works:
push: Triggers the workflow when a push event occurs.
branches: Specifies which branches should trigger the workflow.
main: Runs the workflow only for pushes to the main branch.
actions/checkout: Checks out the repository code onto the runner.
run: Executes shell commands.

💡 Key Takeaway:
The push event helps automate CI processes whenever developers upload code changes, ensuring that every update can be automatically validated
